---
id: org.dxos.plugin.s3
name: S3Plugin
version: 0.1.0
---

A **headless** Composer plugin that stores file blobs in an S3-compatible
bucket — Cloudflare R2, AWS S3 or MinIO. It has no React/UI surfaces. It
contributes two things:

1. A **Connector** entry with a credential form capturing the bucket
   endpoint and an access key pair. On submit the pair is stored as an
   `AccessToken` (`source` = the bucket host, `account` = the access key
   id, `token` = the secret) plus a `Connection` in ECHO.
2. A **`BlobBackend`** registered on the Hypergraph under the storage name
   `s3`, plus the matching `FileCapabilities.Backend` descriptor so it is
   selectable in the file plugin's settings.

The seam is the same one `plugin-wnfs` uses: `plugin-file` owns the
storage-selection UI and `@dxos/echo-client` owns the backend registry, so
neither has to know S3 exists. The credential seam is `CredentialsService`,
so a server-custodied token resolves through EDGE unchanged.

## Extensions

| Term   | URI                     |
| ------ | ----------------------- |
| `type` | `org.dxos.mdl.type@1.0` |
| `feat` | `org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0` |
| `test` | `org.dxos.mdl.test@1.0` |

## Addressing

```mdl
type S3Uri
  desc: >
    A stored blob is addressed `s3://<host>/<key>`, where host is the
    virtual-hosted bucket endpoint and key is `<spaceId>/<contentHash>`.
    The host is also the AccessToken.source the credential is filed under,
    so a read resolves its own credential from the URI with no side table
    mapping blobs to configuration. One Connection therefore addresses
    exactly one bucket, which is the granularity the Connection type
    already models.
  host: string    # media.<account>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
  key: string     # <spaceId>/<contentHash>
```

## Connector

```mdl
feat S3Connector
  desc: >
    Contributes a ConnectorEntry on the ConnectorEvents.Start activation
    event. Non-OAuth, credentialForm only — mirrors plugin-ideogram.
  id: org.dxos.plugin.s3.connector
  source: s3
  label: S3 Storage
  credentialForm:
    schema: S3CredentialForm { endpoint, accessKeyId, secretAccessKey }
    onSubmit: >
      Normalize the endpoint to a bare lowercase host (the dashboard hands
      out a full URL). Fail the Effect — never throw — if any field is
      empty. Build AccessToken { source: host, account: accessKeyId,
      token: secretAccessKey } and Connection { connectorId, accessToken }.
  testConnection: >
    Signed HEAD on a key that should not exist: 404 proves the signature
    is accepted, 403 proves it is not. Needs no write permission and
    leaves nothing behind.
```

## Storage backend

```mdl
feat S3BlobBackend
  desc: >
    Contributes FileCapabilities.Backend and registers a BlobBackend under
    the storage name `s3` on FileEvents.Start (the file plugin's start —
    this plugin has no surface of its own to gate on).
  schemes: [s3]
  put: >
    Resolves the space's single S3 Connection for the destination bucket —
    BlobPutRequest carries no endpoint — then PUTs to
    `<spaceId>/<contentHash>` with a SigV4 Authorization header. The
    content hash the blob manager already computed is reused as the
    payload hash, so nothing is digested twice.
  get/has: >
    Parse the endpoint out of the URI and sign if the space holds a
    credential for that host; otherwise issue the request unsigned. 404
    and 403 both resolve to a miss; anything else rejects, since a
    transport failure is not a missing object.
  getUrl: >
    A presigned GET URL when a credential is available, the plain public
    URL when it is not — so an <img> can load the object without an
    Authorization header it cannot send.
```

## Signing

```mdl
feat SigV4
  desc: >
    AWS Signature Version 4 over WebCrypto, hand-rolled rather than taken
    from @aws-sdk: the SDK signer pulls a credential-provider chain, a
    region resolver and a Node crypto shim that a browser holding one
    static key pair does not need, at two orders of magnitude the size.
  region: >
    Parsed from an AWS hostname; `auto` for R2 and anything else that does
    not encode one. R2 ignores the region but SigV4 still requires a value
    in the credential scope.
  content-length: >
    Deliberately unsigned. It is a forbidden header name, so the browser
    sets it and strips ours; signing it would guarantee a mismatch the
    server reports as a bad signature rather than a missing header.
```

```mdl
test SigV4AwsVectors
  desc: >
    The signer is checked against AWS's own published example signatures
    for both the header-auth and presigned-URL forms, so the tests are
    evidence of correctness rather than of self-consistency.
  vectors:
    - GET Object header auth -> f0e8bdb87c964420e857bd35b5d6ed310bd44f0170aba48dd91039c6036bdb41
    - presigned URL          -> aeeed9bbccd4d02ee5c0109b86d86835f995330da4c265957d157751f604d404
```

## Operator requirements

```mdl
feat BucketConfiguration
  desc: Two things must be true of the bucket or nothing works.
  cors: >
    The bucket's CORS policy must allow the app origin for GET, HEAD and
    PUT. Without it the browser blocks the request before it is sent and
    the failure surfaces as an opaque network error, not a 403.
  trust: >
    The secret access key is replicated into the space, so every member of
    that space can read and write the bucket. Scope the key pair to one
    bucket and treat the space as its trust boundary. A tighter model
    needs EDGE to mint presigned URLs so the key never reaches the client.
```
